# .environment-assumptions-allow — external allow-list for
# scripts/audit-environment-assumptions.sh. Read IN ADDITION to the ALLOW_RULES
# embedded in that script, using the identical syntax:
#
#     # REASON:   <why this literal is genuinely justified>
#     path/to/file            CLASS    substring-of-the-offending-line
#
#     # BASELINE: <a REAL, KNOWN finding, deliberately not fixed yet>
#     path/to/file            CLASS    substring-of-the-offending-line
#
# EVERY rule needs its own marker comment on the line directly above it; the
# gate exits 2 on a rule without one, because an unexplained suppression is
# indistinguishable from a bluff. CLASS and MATCH may each be `*`; PATH may end
# in `*` to prefix-match.
#
# ==============================================================================
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ==============================================================================
# Until 2026-09-01 this gate enumerated files with a bare `git ls-files` at the
# umbrella root. A submodule appears there as a GITLINK — one directory entry,
# no contents — and the `[ -f ... ]` filter dropped it. MEASURED blind spot:
# `--list` reported 179 files, ZERO of them inside any of the nine declared
# submodules, across 9 repositories and ~1 350 additional source files.
#
# Switching the fleet sweep on surfaced 197 (file, class) findings that ALREADY
# EXISTED and that the gate could not see. They are recorded here, NOT fixed and
# NOT excused:
#
#   * Fixing them is out of scope for the change that made them visible, and
#     several of those trees have other agents working in them right now.
#   * They live in OTHER repositories. §11.4.29 forbids editing a tree this
#     repository does not author from here; a fix lands in the submodule's own
#     upstream and returns as a gitlink bump.
#   * NOTHING BELOW ASSERTS A FINDING IS A DEFECT, and nothing below asserts it
#     is a false positive. Nobody has triaged them. That untriaged state IS the
#     debt being recorded — §11.4.6 forbids reporting a state you have not
#     verified, in either direction. Spot-checking suggests the population is
#     mixed: `net.Listen("tcp", "127.0.0.1:0")` in a Go test is the CORRECT
#     portable idiom, while `thinker.local` baked into a test fixture is the
#     same operator-machine assumption this gate exists to catch.
#
# SCOPE OF EACH ENTRY. One (file, class) pair. NOT a whole submodule, NOT a
# whole file: a NEW finding of a DIFFERENT class in an already-baselined file
# still fails, and any finding in any not-listed file still fails.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE. `submodules/superspec` (upstream WangX0111) is THIRD-PARTY.
# Its findings are neither baselined nor suppressed — the gate REPORTS them as
# an out-of-scope NOTE on every run and does not fail on them. `design-toolkit`
# is clean and needs no entry.
#
# HONEST BOUNDARY (§11.4.6) ON THESE ENTRIES. The gate's own failure text asks a
# BASELINE to "carry an AUDIT.md finding id". These entries do NOT have one:
# docs/environment-adaptability/AUDIT.md is outside the set of files the change
# that produced this ledger was permitted to touch. This dated, per-(file,class)
# ledger IS the record for them; assigning F-numbers in AUDIT.md is owed work,
# and saying so is preferable to inventing ids that point at nothing.
#
# Re-derive the live list at any time:
#     bash scripts/audit-environment-assumptions.sh
#     bash scripts/audit-environment-assumptions.sh --list   # per-repo universe
# ==============================================================================


